  <section class="panel active" id="brief" role="tabpanel"><div class="card glance"><h2>At a glance</h2><ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> 70 paid sprint sales from non-customers by 10 Nov (2 so far)</li><li><strong>Who:</strong> marketers 5 to 15 years in, on the whole CXL list and Peep's LinkedIn · <strong>Not for:</strong> beginners, coupon traffic</li><li><strong>Offer:</strong> the one-week sprint, $799, three live builds → <strong>CTA:</strong> buy a seat (subscribers: enroll)</li><li><strong>Angle:</strong> learn by doing: you leave the week with three workflows that run on your own marketing</li><li><strong>Channels:</strong> email, LinkedIn, YouTube Shorts, the sprint page, team-offer handoff</li><li><strong>Gate 1:</strong> passes; the offer only lands if every send shows a build and states the price</li></ul></div><div class="secs"><div class="sec"><h3>Goal</h3><ul><li><strong>KPI:</strong> paid sprint sales from non-customers: one-off seats plus new subscriptions this campaign brings in</li><li><strong>Target:</strong> 70 by 10 Nov 2026, the first workshop</li><li><strong>Baseline:</strong> 2 paid seats, none since 2 Oct (Metorik)</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Approach</h3><p>Get marketers on the CXL list and Peep's LinkedIn audience to buy the one-week sprint by showing members' working builds in about four emails a week, closing on a BFCM week where the discounted subscription includes it.</p></div><div class="sec"><h3>Audience</h3><ul><li><strong>Include:</strong> individual marketers levelling up (icp.md); employer-paid buyers on 37 company domains (icp.md); one-off course buyers on a company domain (icp.md)</li><li><strong>Also told, not counted:</strong> subscribers and six-week members get an enroll link; team leads get the $3,999 offer via sales</li><li><strong>Exclude:</strong> anyone who already bought the sprint; beginners (to courses); coupon and partner traffic</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> about 38,500 non-customers; about 47,000 on the full list (Customer.io)</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Offer</h3><ul><li><strong>Offer:</strong> three 90-minute live builds, 10 to 12 Nov, a month in the community, refund until the first workshop, $799</li><li><strong>Destination:</strong> cxl.com/institute/ai-native-marketer/</li><li><strong>CTA:</strong> buy the sprint seat; every send also carries "Already a subscriber? Enroll here"</li><li><strong>From 2 Nov (BFCM):</strong> the subscription at 25% off, $1,199, includes this sprint and January's</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Messaging</h3><div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Angle</th><th>Proof</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Learn by doing: three workflows you keep</td><td>5 member demos in Circle, 6 to 8 Oct; Kerry M.: "I already have a workflow live"</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>It fits inside your job: each build is 90 minutes plus 1 to 2 hours</td><td>Kerry M., 6 Oct email (per build, never a weekly total)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Drafts that start on brand and check their own claims</td><td>42% get generic output, 37% hallucinated data (Wynter, N=80)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A fixed week with a group gets it done</td><td>"easy to put off in favor of more urgent tasks" (Tai, Wynter)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>AI skills are now in job descriptions</td><td>AI in 43% of Q1 listings (Q3 study, not yet filed in <code>raw/</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="sec"><h3>Buying stage</h3><ul><li><strong>Stage:</strong> solution aware: they use AI daily, the output disappoints, and they are weighing options</li><li><strong>Their words:</strong> "I don't need more tips, I need the time to actually dedicate to building." (Alex, Wynter)</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Sales handoff</h3><ul><li><strong>Lead:</strong> a team hand-raise: the team offer message on the page, or a question about team seats, invoices, VAT or a PO</li><li><strong>Handoff:</strong> Brian and Kimberly, in Intercom, within one working day; individual seats are self-serve</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Measurement</h3><ul><li><strong>Tracking:</strong> UTMs on every link, <code>utm_campaign=ai-native-sprint-nov26</code>; one test purchase before 22 Oct</li><li><strong>Kill rule:</strong> fewer than 15 paid by Mon 26 Oct: change the angle and the offer before the 27 Oct send</li><li><strong>Scale rule:</strong> 28 or more by Sun 1 Nov: add a final-week send and a second Peep post</li></ul></div><div class="sec"><h3>Constraints</h3><ul><li>About four sends a week, 9 Oct to 9 Nov; every send mentions the sprint</li><li>No paid media for the sprint; the webinar series and app ads get their own linked brief</li><li>BFCM starts 2 Nov; the Company brain workshop is 3 Nov (promos 21 and 26 Oct)</li><li>Peep's narrative is pending; drafts don't wait for it</li></ul></div></div><details><summary>Evidence and sources</summary><ul><li><strong>Why 70:</strong> set by Hesh and Brian from cohort modelling toward $4M net revenue. Open: the 19 Sep plan's $4M maths needed about 600 to 700 buyers per intake (<code>daily-logs/2026-09-19-convo.md</code>). 70 is 2.7 times the last cohort and about a tenth of that; the gap belongs in the cohort model.</li><li><strong>Baseline:</strong> 2 paid seats, both 30 Sep to 2 Oct (Metorik, product 9155558, to 8 Oct). The six-week cohort sold 26 paid seats, $17,880 net, 24 Aug to 1 Oct, every order a single seat; 60% of units in the last 9 days before the start (Metorik, product 9153380).</li><li><strong>Counting the KPI:</strong> one-off seats are Metorik units of product 9155558, net of refunds at 10 Nov. Attributable subscriptions are new orders, 8 Oct to 10 Nov, with the sprint as the initial interest product in the order meta; renewals never count. If Metorik can't filter on that key, read it from the WooCommerce order. Team offers have no product, so they are counted by hand and reported apart from the 70.</li><li><strong>Audience detail:</strong> Buyer 1 is a Marketing Manager, Demand Gen, Growth, PMM or Content Manager (icp.md, Two buyers). Employer-paid: 37 domains with 2 or more individual buyers, 89 people. One-off course buyers on a company domain: about 300 (icp.md, scorecard). Six-week members: 221 active. Active subscriptions: 1,486 (Metorik, 8 Oct); Customer.io counts 2,917 personal subscribers and 2,617 team seats (the two count differently). One-off customers with no active subscription: 5,229.</li><li><strong>Why customers are included:</strong> earlier promos went to 38,551 because customers were excluded; this launch goes to them too, so subscribers use what they pay for (Hesh, 9 Oct).</li><li><strong>Email baseline:</strong> cohort and sprint promos since 24 Aug: 38,000 to 48,000 delivered, 5% to 9% human opens, 0.2% to 0.6% human clicks. Sprint promo #1 (5 Oct): 38,286 delivered, 8.8% open, 0.30% click, 1 conversion in 21 days (Customer.io pull).</li><li><strong>Reach math (inferred):</strong> about 18 sends to about 38,500 at 0.30% click is roughly 2,100 clicks; 68 more sales needs about 1 in 31 clickers to buy. The six-week cohort ran at about 1 in 35. Each send costs about 50 to 130 unsubscribes.</li><li><strong>Pace line (inferred):</strong> the six-week cohort sold 40% of its seats before its last 9 days; for 70, about 28 by 1 Nov.</li><li><strong>Why the offer is weak today:</strong> the page converts at 0.06% of sessions, a quarter of the site average (positioning-messaging.md, Evidence); about 53% want to see a build first and about 57% are unclear on price (Wynter, 21 Sep 2026).</li><li><strong>Angle sources:</strong> angle 1: <code>raw/voc/circle-cohort-demos-2026-10-08.md</code>; "hands-on projects" is the first choice for how they learn, 31 of 80 (Wynter May 2026); 11.7% of 2,746 chose CXL for practical reasons (welcome survey). Angle 2: <code>raw/voc/kerry-m-time-feedback-2026-10-07.md</code>; measured on the six-week format, one workshop a week. Angle 3: Ella, "the output often sounds generic or repetitive" (icp.md); Kerry M.: "About half were usable out of the box". Angle 4: positioning-messaging.md, Pillar 3; "second job" quoted by 5 of 30 (Wynter 17 and 21 Sep). Angle 5: Tarek's email plan (8 Oct newsletter), Q3 report artifact 2upb99YDr9GvpR6Tw7VPny.</li><li><strong>Messaging anchors:</strong> OKM "Learn by doing. Leave with AI workflows that work on your marketing." Differentiator: "Issued when the workflow runs, not when the video ends." (positioning-messaging.md). Peep Laja is the founder voice; until his narrative lands, Tarek's angle stands: "You can become AI native in one week".</li><li><strong>Buyer's thought, in full:</strong> "I don't need more tips, I need the time to actually dedicate to building -- learn by doing, not by skimming more content about what to do. Actually do it." (Alex, Marketing Operations Strategist, Wynter, 21 Aug 2026). What moves them: seeing a build from a marketer like them, knowing the time it takes, seeing the price.</li><li><strong>Sales detail:</strong> a one-page rep briefing with the $3,999 terms, the subscription alternative, and the invoice and PO process (209 of 560 Intercom billing threads, Jan to Aug 2026, are invoice, VAT and PO requests, icp.md). No sequence; a short reply template.</li><li><strong>Tracking detail:</strong> <code>utm_source</code> = customer.io, linkedin or youtube; <code>utm_medium</code> = email or social; <code>utm_content</code> = the send or post slug. Weekly report on Mondays: units against the pace line, by source.</li><li><strong>Cadence detail:</strong> newsletters carry a sprint plug; workshop emails lead with the sprint and treat the workshop as the footnote; emails already in automation 435 get the subscriber CTA added; Tarek's plan updated to match (Hesh, 9 Oct).</li><li><strong>Assets:</strong> live sprint page; sprint promo #1 (5 Oct) and workshop promos with a plug (6, 9, 13, 14 Oct); promos #2 to #5 (22 Oct, 27 Oct, 2 Nov, 9 Nov) to come; 5 Circle demos; the Q3 job description study.</li><li><strong>Claims and sign-off:</strong> member quotes are cleared under the terms and conditions (Hesh, 9 Oct). Angle 2 states per-build time only. The pricing FAQ's "We don't do discounts" stays as is (Hesh, 9 Oct). The working-workflow guarantee has four conditions: quote them or leave it out. Voice: cxl-plugin voice guide and vocabulary; CXL, never "CXL Institute".</li><li><strong>Linked brief:</strong> the webinar series runs the week before the sprint, 2 to 3 sessions, avoiding 3 Nov, with the team AI assessment app as the lead magnet and CPL ads. Benchmark: the n8n webinar week brought about 2,000 emails on about $1,000 of ads and $10,000 to $13,000 from about 7 customers. Expect single-digit sprint sales from it (inferred).</li><li><strong>Sunset automation 428:</strong> rules changed and a wait-forever block added (Hesh, 9 Oct). The 8 Oct pull still showed the OR rule and 846 deletions in 45 days; the next pull should confirm the fix took.</li><li><strong>Hesh's answers, 8 and 9 Oct:</strong> target 70 set with Brian; subscribers get an enroll link; six-week members and subscribers included; Brian and Kimberly own team leads; about four sends a week; the 70 come from non-customers; BFCM starts 2 Nov with the subscription at 25% off including this and the January sprint; FAQ unchanged; Peep confirmed.</li></ul></details><details><summary>Gate 1: all 14 checks pass</summary><div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Check</th><th>Pass?</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>One KPI, a number, a date</td><td>Pass</td><td>70 paid sprint sales by 10 Nov</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The KPI is a buying action</td><td>Pass</td><td>Purchases</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The KPI can be tracked today</td><td>Pass, one fix</td><td>Confirm Metorik can filter subscriptions on the initial interest product</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Approach fits one sentence</td><td>Pass</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Include and exclude lists</td><td>Pass</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Every angle has proof</td><td>Pass</td><td>Angle 2 scoped to per-build time</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Proof from customers or third parties</td><td>Pass</td><td>Circle demos, Wynter, Kerry M.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Buying stage with their words</td><td>Pass</td><td>Alex, Wynter</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Would they miss it</td><td>Pass, flagged</td><td>Only if sends show a build and state the price</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The ask matches the stage</td><td>Pass</td><td>Direct buy, refund until the first workshop</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Handoff: person, place, time</td><td>Pass</td><td>Brian and Kimberly, Intercom, one working day</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Sales agreed the lead definition</td><td>Pass</td><td>Hesh, 9 Oct</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Kill and scale rules</td><td>Pass</td><td>15 by 26 Oct, 28 by 1 Nov</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Consistent with the positioning</td><td>Pass</td><td>OKM and Pillars 1 to 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></section>
