</span><span class="pill " title="Claude">Claude</span></li></ol></details><div class="group-title">Pieces</div><details><summary>H1 · Hesh · Fri 9 Oct · angle 1</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> Five marketers built a marketing brain on Tuesday. By Thursday, all five had posted it working.</p>
<p>On 6 October we ran the first build of our AI native program: live online workshops with a fixed group and fixed dates.</p>
<p>The build was a marketing brain. One document your AI tools read before they write anything: your customer, your positioning, your voice, your proof. So drafts start on brand instead of generic.</p>
<p>Within two days, five members had posted their working versions in the community.</p>
<p>What they said wasn't all praise, and that's the useful part.</p>
<p>Alfonso: "It's impressive how much clarity came out of only the second workshop."</p>
<p>Kerry: "About half were usable out of the box and half need more revisions, but it's a stronger set and a great shortcut."</p>
<p>Pavlo, on his customer profile: "So the whole thing is a hypothesis with a clear list of what to verify."</p>
<p>That's what I want from AI work: a first draft you can check, and a list of what to verify.</p>
<p>Next group builds three of these in one week, 10 to 12 November. Link in the comments.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Tags:</strong> Alfonso v. Z., Kerry McDonough, Pavlo A. (if connected)</li><li><strong>Words:</strong> 176</li></ul></details><details><summary>T1 · Tarek · Mon 12 Oct · angle 5</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> We read the AI requirements in LinkedIn marketing job posts for three quarters. Q3 is where it changed.</p>
<p>Every quarter we scrape a batch of marketing job descriptions from LinkedIn and read what they ask for.</p>
<p>In Q1, AI showed up in 43% of 1,000 listings. In Q3, mentions of AI went up across the board: AI as a listed requirement, more automation, and phrases like "AI-native" in the role itself.</p>
<p>[PLACEHOLDER: Q3 figure and sample size, from Tarek's report]</p>
<p>What stood out to me is how specific the asks are getting. Not "familiar with AI tools". Workflows: research, campaign drafting, reporting.</p>
<p>Which is the gap I see most. Plenty of marketers use ChatGPT every day. Far fewer can show a workflow they built that runs on their own data.</p>
<p>Full report in the comments. And if you'd rather build one than read about it, our next sprint is 10 to 12 November.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Tags:</strong> none</li><li><strong>Words:</strong> about 150 with the Q3 figure</li></ul></details><details><summary>H2 · Hesh · Tue 13 Oct · angle 3</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> Tonight we build a campaign engine that refuses to publish a claim it can't source.</p>
<p>The most common complaint we hear about AI drafts: they're plausible, and wrong.</p>
<p>"It's untrustworthy and checking mistakes is extremely time consuming, because the mistakes are so plausible." A content marketing manager said that in a panel we ran in May.</p>
<p>So the campaign engine we build tonight has one rule before anything else: every claim in a draft needs a source from your own files. No source, it stops and flags it for you.</p>
<p>One brief in. Emails, social and sequences out. With sources you can check, and a check against your brand voice.</p>
<p>It's the second build in our program. Tonight it runs as a standalone workshop. On 11 November it runs as day two of the one-week sprint.</p>
<p>What's the AI mistake you've caught too late?</p>
<ul><li><strong>Tags:</strong> Tyler Durman (designed the build)</li><li><strong>Words:</strong> 150</li></ul></details><details><summary>P1 · Peep · Wed 14 Oct · angle 4 (draft for Peep to rewrite in his voice)</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> Most AI training fails for a boring reason: it's never urgent.</p>
<p>Nobody decides not to learn AI. It just loses to this week's deadline, every week.</p>
<p>When we tested our sprint page with marketers, one line kept coming back. A product marketing manager put it this way: "that adds some external pressure... easy to put off in favor of more urgent tasks."</p>
<p>That's why we built the sprint around fixed dates and a fixed group. Three evenings, 10 to 12 November. One build a night: a marketing brain, a campaign engine, a reporting agent. You demo each one working before it counts.</p>
<p>A week with an end, instead of a library you'll get to someday.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Tags:</strong> none</li><li><strong>Words:</strong> 108</li><li><strong>Note for Peep:</strong> your narrative replaces this angle once you land it; the facts above are from the brief.</li></ul></details><details><summary>T2 · Tarek · Wed 14 Oct · angle 3</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> 42% of marketers say their AI output is too generic. Here's where that comes from.</p>
<p>In May we ran a panel of 80 B2B marketers with Wynter. Asked what goes wrong with AI at work, 42% said generic output. 37% said hallucinated data.</p>
<p>One of them: "the output often sounds generic or repetitive, so I end up rewriting a lot manually."</p>
<p>Another: "I still have to keep restating the strategy, reminding it of the brand voice, copying outputs between tools."</p>
<p>Both point at the same cause. The AI doesn't know your customer, your positioning or your voice, so it guesses. Every chat starts from zero.</p>
<p>What fixes it is context the AI reads every time: one document with your customer, positioning, voice and proof. We call it a marketing brain, and it's the first thing members build.</p>
<p>Next sprint: 10 to 12 November. Link below.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Tags:</strong> Wynter</li><li><strong>Words:</strong> 150</li></ul></details><details><summary>H3 · Hesh · Thu 15 Oct · angle 2</summary><p><strong>Hook:</strong> We asked a member how long our program really takes. Her answer was more useful than ours.</p>
<p>Our page says "an average of 1 hour a day" across the prep week and the sprint week. I wanted to hear it from inside.</p>
