<h3>Step 1 · Is it worth it (default)</h3>
<p>Run as a test, not a channel commitment. The n8n week is the benchmark: about $1,000 for about 2,000 emails, about $0.50 an email. Past paid traffic to the sprint page barely sold (PMax 3,199 sessions, 0 purchases; Demand Gen $3,262, 0 conversions; positioning-messaging.md, Evidence), so the KPI here is cost per email, never purchases.</p>
<h3>Step 2 · Budget against the metric</h3>
<p>Wilcox: about $1,000 judges clicks, $5,000 or more judges CPL. <strong>Default: $1,000 to $1,500 across both platforms, matching n8n</strong>, read as cost per email with the CPL verdict directional. Raise to $5,000 if you want a CPL verdict you can scale on.</p>
<h3>Step 3 · Tracking first</h3>
<ul><li>Google Ads conversion and Meta Pixel plus Conversions API on the webinar and app pages; conversion = email submitted.</li><li>UTMs lowercase: <code>utm_source=google|meta&amp;utm_medium=paid&amp;utm_campaign=ai-native-webinar-nov26&amp;utm_content=&lt;ad id&gt;</code> (the linked brief's campaign slug).</li><li>Flag from the brain: Google Ads counts two primary purchase conversions (risk of double counting). Set the email submit as the only primary conversion for these campaigns.</li></ul>
<h3>Step 4 · Platform defaults (adapted from LinkedIn)</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Google Search:</strong> Search network only (no Display, no Search Partners), manual or max-conversions with a CPA cap, exact and phrase match only, location by country list, not "interest in".</li><li><strong>Meta:</strong> Advantage+ audience off for the first test, manual placements (Feed, Reels, Stories), Leads objective to the landing page, not instant forms (keep the email in our tools).</li></ul>
<h3>Step 5 · Test audiences</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Google Search, by intent:</strong> "ai readiness assessment", "marketing team ai skills", "ai marketing training", "holiday marketing ai" (volumes to check in Ahrefs before launch).</li><li><strong>Meta:</strong> (a) job titles and interests for B2B marketing roles; (b) 1% lookalike of the CXL customer list; (c) retargeting site visitors in the last 30 days who are not on the list. One ad set per audience.</li></ul>
<h3>Step 6 · Exclusions</h3>
<p>Current and past customers (customer list upload), the 47,000 already on the newsletter list, CXL employees, competitors. Brief exclusions apply.</p>
<h3>Step 7 · Objective</h3>
<p>Google: conversions (email submit). Meta: leads to the website. Compare objectives only in separate tests.</p>
<h3>Step 8 · Three stages</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Cold (Meta a, b; Google):</strong> the app: "How ready is your marketing team for AI?" Ungated partial result, gated full report.</li><li><strong>Engaged (Meta c):</strong> the webinar series registration.</li><li><strong>Converted:</strong> out of ads; into the sprint emails.</li></ul>
<h3>Step 9 · What the first test changes (default)</h3>
<p>Offer first: <strong>app versus webinar</strong> as the landing destination, same audience, same creative style. Then creative, then copy.</p>
<h3>Step 11 · Score before running</h3>
<p>Score each ad 1 to 5 on their words, outcome-led and voice (Nick Christensen's ad-copy rule); cut anything under 4. To run in the linked brief once the app and sessions are final.</p>
